<section class="app-page">

  <h1 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-3xl font-bold text-brand-text">
    {% icon "fa-solid-network-wired", "h-7 w-7 text-brand-accent" %}
    <span>How this is built</span>
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-brand-muted">
    Three deployables, four data stores and five upstreams. This page is the map of what runs
    where, what holds which data, and what every endpoint is for.
  </p>

  {# --- the flow -----------------------------------------------------------
     Drawn rather than described because the one thing people get wrong about this setup is which
     box talks to which, and a list of hostnames does not answer that. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="flow-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="flow-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Where a request goes</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      The two sites are static files. Everything that needs a key, a database or a verified identity
      goes through the API.
    </p>

    <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
      {% include "chrome/architecture.njk" %}
    </div>

    {# This paragraph used to read "the browser never calls an upstream directly", which was a
       rule rather than a description until the maps arrived, and then it was simply false. A page
       that states its own rules has to change them in the same commit as the code, so here is the
       narrower claim that is still true, and the exception said out loud rather than hidden by a
       word like "generally". #}
    <p class="hint mt-3 max-w-prose">
      Everything that needs a key, a database or an identity goes through the API: two of the five
      upstreams need a credential, and a key in a page is a key anyone can read with View Source.
      The one thing that does not is the basemap. Map tiles are fetched one per square as you pan,
      so your browser asks OpenFreeMap for them directly — proxying a few hundred of them a session
      would mean running a tile server rather than forwarding a call. It is the only upstream your
      browser contacts itself — which is why the diagram names it as an exception rather than drawing
      it: the arrow would leave from the top of the picture rather than the bottom. The upstream list
      below says what that costs.
    </p>
  </section>

  {# --- the deployables ---------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="deploy-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="deploy-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The three deployables</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Two of them are built from the same repository by two Eleventy configurations that share one
      set of includes and one stylesheet, so both draw their chrome from the same implementation.
    </p>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="doc-method">Site</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-text">
          <strong>tzortzoglou.eu</strong> — the public portfolio. Eleventy, output to
          <code>_site/</code>, served by GitHub Pages. Loads no Firebase at all.
        </span>
        <span class="doc-role">static</span>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="doc-method">App</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-text">
          <strong>app.tzortzoglou.eu</strong> — the signed-in app. A second Eleventy build of the
          same repository, output to <code>_app/</code>. The pages are static; what they show
          comes from the API, which is what checks who you are.
        </span>
        <span class="doc-role">static</span>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-row">
        <span class="doc-method">API</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-text">
          <strong>api.tzortzoglou.eu</strong> — Express, in its own private repository. Holds every
          key and is the only thing that talks to a database.
        </span>
        <span class="doc-role">node</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {# --- where data lives ----------------------------------------------------
     The section this page mostly exists for. "The database" sounds like one thing and is four,
     and three of the four are not the one people mean. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="data-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="data-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Where data lives</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Four places, and they hold very different things. The weather, the departures and the
      Formula 1 data are not stored anywhere.
    </p>

    <ul class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Firebase Authentication</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          Email addresses and password hashes. The browser trades a password for a signed token, and
          the API only ever sees the token. Deleting an account here is what actually stops somebody
          signing in; everything else is a record about them.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Firestore</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          One document per account: name, role, approval state, home location, home stop. The role
          is read from here on <em>every single request</em>. It is a separate store from the one
          above, which is why an account can exist with no document and why erasure removes both.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Postgres</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          Four tables: contact submissions, a row per process start, which migrations have run, and
          one row per page view — path, which of the two properties, the referring host and the
          time, and deliberately nothing else. No address, no cookie, no identifier of any kind,
          and not the full referring URL, only its host.
          Browsable on
          <a href="/admin/data/" class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">the data view</a>
          if you are an admin. A contact submission can be deleted one row at a time from
          <a href="/admin/messages/" class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">Messages</a>,
          or all of one person's at once by erasure.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Memory</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-1">
          Every upstream answer is cached in the API process for between 45 seconds and six hours,
          and lost on restart. A bus or a race lives in memory and nowhere else, so there is no
          history of either to browse.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    {# Added the day erasure was built, because until then this section described four stores
       nothing could take anything out of, and a page called "where data lives" that cannot say
       how data leaves is only half a map. The last two sentences are the ones that matter: the
       emailed copy is real, it is outside all four stores, and no endpoint here reaches it. #}
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mt-4 max-w-prose">
      Until recently nothing in this system could take a person out of any of them. It can now:
      erasure takes one email address and removes that address's contact messages from Postgres,
      its account document from Firestore and its sign-in from Firebase Authentication — then
      answers with a receipt of what it actually removed, rather than a success message. One
      action across three stores, because that is how far a person is spread: deleting from one
      leaves the other two still holding them.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mt-3 max-w-prose">
      There is a fifth place, and it is the honest part of this page. A message sent through the
      contact form is stored <em>and</em> emailed, so a copy sits in a mailbox and in the mail
      provider's delivery log. Neither is a store this code owns, and nothing in the endpoint list
      below reaches into them. The erasure preview and the receipt both say so, in the API's own
      words, rather than reporting a deletion that sounds wider than the one that happened.
    </p>

    {# The two properties count differently, and a reader who noticed the banner on one site and
       not on the other deserves the reason rather than the appearance of an oversight. #}
    <h3 class="mt-8 font-semibold text-brand-text">Why the two sites count differently</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mt-2 max-w-prose">
      The public site counts only after you say yes. The banner is opt-in, declining means nothing
      is counted at all, and that has not changed — what changed is only where the count goes: to
      the API above rather than to a third party.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mt-3 max-w-prose">
      This app counts every page load, with no banner. It is behind a login, everyone here is a
      known account holder, and counting loads of your own service for the running of that service
      is operational telemetry rather than audience measurement — the legal basis is different, so
      the consent is too. The line that keeps that honest is being told, which is what this
      paragraph and <a href="https://tzortzoglou.eu/privacy/" rel="noopener"
        class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">the privacy notice</a> are for; a
      count nobody is told about would be the same count with the honesty taken out.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mt-3 max-w-prose">
      "Everyone here is a known account holder" has to be true of the code, not only of the intent,
      and for a while it was not: these pages are static files that anyone can fetch, and the
      sign-in is a redirect JavaScript performs once one has loaded — so a stranger opening
      <code>/transit/</code> was counted on the way to being bounced. The beacon now fires from
      <code>shell.js</code> only after <code>/me</code> has confirmed the session, which is the
      earliest moment that sentence means anything. Not signed in, not counted.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mt-3 max-w-prose">
      Erasure does not reach this table, and there is nothing in it to reach. A view row names
      nobody: there is no account on it, no address, and nothing to join it to a person with, which
      is the point rather than an omission.
    </p>
  </section>

  {# --- endpoints ----------------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="api-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="api-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The endpoints</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Every route the API serves, and the lowest role that may call it, grouped and folded away
      on a narrow screen. Each one re-reads your role
      and approval from Firestore before doing anything, because a token proves who signed in, not
      what they may do now.
    </p>

    {# Fetched rather than built into this page, and this is the reason:
       app.tzortzoglou.eu is a directory of static files. Its sign-in is a redirect in
       JavaScript, which decides what is drawn and guards nothing at all - every page here is
       served in full to anyone who asks for the URL. A complete list of endpoints and the role
       each one needs is the most useful thing on this page to somebody who has no account, so
       it is the one part that is not in the HTML. It comes from the API, which asks who you are.

       It also stopped being a hand-kept copy in the process. The list now lives beside the
       router in the API repository, and that repository's test suite fails if the two disagree
       in either direction. #}
    <div id="endpoints" class="mt-4"></div>
    <p id="endpointsNote" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="hint mt-4">Loading the endpoint list…</p>

  </section>

  {# --- upstreams ------------------------------------------------------------
     The section that replaced an entity-relationship diagram of three tables nobody stores
     anything interesting in. "We call an API" describes nothing; which endpoint, how often it
     may be called, and whose field became which of ours is where the work actually is. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="up-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="up-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The five upstreams, in detail</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-6 max-w-prose">
      Four of them are proxied. Two of those need a credential, which has to stay on the server;
      the other two so that one call serves every reader. The fifth is the basemap, and it is not
      proxied at all — your browser fetches its tiles itself, which makes it the only one of
      the five your browser has ever heard of. Each one below lists what is called, how long an answer
      is held, and how their field names become ours.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-6 max-w-prose">
      In the mapping tables, theirs is on the left and ours on the right. A row marked
      <em>derived</em> is something nobody sent: it is worked out here, and those rows are the
      decisions rather than the transport.
    </p>

    {%- for up in upstreams %}
    <details class="group card mb-4" {% if loop.first %}open{% endif %}>
      <summary class="flex cursor-pointer select-none flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1
        list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden focus-visible:outline-none
        focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-brand-accent rounded">
        <span class="text-base font-bold text-brand-text">{{ up.name }}</span>
        <span class="doc-role">{% if up.key %}key{% else %}no key{% endif %}</span>
        <span class="flex-1 text-xs text-brand-muted">held {{ up.cache }}</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted transition-transform duration-150 group-open:rotate-180" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
      </summary>

      <p class="hint mt-2 max-w-prose">{{ up.what }}</p>

      <h3 class="mt-5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-muted">What is called</h3>
      <div class="mt-2">
        {%- for call in up.calls %}
        <div class="doc-row">
          <code class="text-xs text-brand-text">{{ call.path }}</code>
          <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted">{{ call.note }}</span>
        </div>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-muted">
        How it is mapped
      </h3>
      <div class="mt-2">
        {%- for row in up.mapping %}
        <div class="map-row">
          <span class="map-from">{{ row[0] }}</span>
          <span class="shrink-0 text-brand-muted" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="map-to">
            {{ row[1] }}{% if row[2] %} <span class="text-[10px] font-sans uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-accent">derived</span>{% endif %}
          </span>
        </div>
        {#- An upstream that answers with images has no field names to rename, and a table with no
            rows in it under a heading that promises one is worse than a sentence. #}
        {%- else %}
        <p class="hint max-w-prose">{{ up.mappingNote }}</p>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-muted">Worth knowing</h3>
      <p class="hint mt-1 max-w-prose">{{ up.why }}</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
        {%- for note in up.notes %}
        <li class="text-sm text-brand-muted max-w-prose">{{ note }}</li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
    {%- endfor %}

    <p class="hint mt-4 max-w-prose">
      The two credentials are read from the environment on the server and stay out of every
      response, error message and log line. Upstream errors are reported by status code only,
      because an error body can echo the request back, and the request carries the key.
    </p>
  </section>

  {# --- conventions ---------------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="rules-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="rules-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Rules the code follows</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Each of these is here because breaking it caused a real bug in this codebase.
    </p>
    <ul class="card space-y-3 text-sm text-brand-text">
      <li>
        <strong>Check the type before coercing a number.</strong> <code>Number(null)</code> is
        <code>0</code>, <code>Number([])</code> is <code>0</code>, and a query string can produce an
        array without being asked. Every parser guards on <code>typeof</code> first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An unknown role is the lowest role.</strong> Role names are an explicit list, not
        case-folding. Anything unrecognised resolves to the most restrictive answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A comment may not claim a control the code does not have.</strong> If the code
        cannot be made true, the comment changes in the same commit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A signal that was never about the thing being checked proves nothing about it.</strong>
        A green test suite that never loads a module says nothing about that module.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never invent data on a dashboard.</strong> A panel with no source behind it says so.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</section>
